# The functions change what they are handed and say nothing, but a change is in the arrow whatever the signature says:
# each step that changes a value takes it as it was and makes it at its own moment, held by the name the caller holds
# it by. Inside a step, an operation that changes the value it runs on is a step of its own (`this.rows.push(..)`,
# `lines.push(s)`, `m.set(..)`). `takeFrom` and `takeVictim` change the ledger they are called on and the one they are
# handed (`other.rows.length = 0`, in their own body): two values each. `fileAll(ledger.rows)` is handed the ledger (a
# projection is glued into the call around it) and files the expenses in its rows, so the ledger changes. `rename` is
# handed an object literal. What `rename` gives goes back to `main`, what `takeFrom` and `takeVictim` give is dropped.
route ledger.ledger.main
step main/Ledger()
step main/Ledger()#2
step main/Ledger.takeFrom
step main/Ledger.takeFrom/this.rows.push
step main/Ledger.takeVictim
step main/Ledger.takeVictim/this.rows.push
step main/addLine
step main/addLine/push
step main/count
step main/count/set
step main/fileAll
step main/fileAll/for e of expenses
step main/rename

line entry > main call
line main/Ledger() > Ledger·ledger@main/Ledger() value
line main/Ledger()#2 > Ledger·other@main/Ledger()#2 value
line Ledger·other@main/Ledger()#2 > main/Ledger.takeFrom/this.rows.push value
line main/Ledger.takeFrom > Ledger·ledger@main/Ledger.takeFrom value
known #128 line Ledger·ledger@main/Ledger.takeFrom > main/Ledger.takeVictim/this.rows.push value # the push runs on this.rows, which the reader does not hand
line main/Ledger.takeVictim > Ledger·ledger@main/Ledger.takeVictim value
line main/addLine/push > _·lines@main/addLine/push value
line main/count/set > _·m@main/count/set value
line main/fileAll/for e of expenses > Expense·e@main/fileAll/for e of expenses value
line Expense·e@main/fileAll/for e of expenses > main/fileAll value       # `e.category = ..` in the body
line main/rename > Expense@main/rename value

home Ledger·ledger@main/Ledger() main
home Ledger·other@main/Ledger()#2 main
home Ledger·ledger@main/Ledger.takeFrom main
home Ledger·ledger@main/Ledger.takeVictim main
home _·lines@main/addLine/push main/addLine
home _·m@main/count/set main/count
home Expense·e@main/fileAll/for e of expenses main/fileAll
home Expense@main/rename main

side entry left

# A step handed a value through a projection takes, beside it, its parameter's type from beyond, and what it changes is
# that type at its moment, not the value it was handed: `fileAll(ledger.rows)` changes the ledger
